// `bytes` can shrink in place, from either end.
//
// Before this it could only grow: `push`, `extend`, and `clear` back to
// nothing. A read buffer that had consumed a prefix had to build a copy
// with `substr` and throw the original away, which is an allocation and a
// copy per read.
//
// The two disagree about an argument past the end, on purpose. `truncate(n)`
// asks to be at most `n` long, which a shorter buffer already is; the trap
// test beside this one shows `drop_front(n)` refusing to remove bytes that
// are not there.
bytes b = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
b.truncate(3);
print(b.hex());
// Already that short: nothing happens, and nothing traps.
b.truncate(3);
b.truncate(9);
print(b.size());
b.drop_front(1);
print(b.hex());
// Down to empty from either end.
b.drop_front(2);
print(b.size());
// The allocation survives both, as it does `clear`: the next push refills
// the storage that is already there.
b.push(255);
print(b.hex());
// An empty buffer takes either without complaint.
bytes empty = [];
empty.truncate(0);
empty.drop_front(0);
print(empty.size());
// Shrinking is in place, so a second name for the same bytes sees it.
bytes shared = [9, 8, 7];
bytes alias = shared;
shared.drop_front(1);
print(alias.hex());
